<appendix xml:id="ha-concordance">
  <title>Terminology and hypothesis comparisons</title>
  <section xml:id="ha-concordance-language">
    <title>Names and structures</title>
    <p>This appendix collects distinctions used in the chapters. Each entry refers to the definition in the main text; it does not replace its hypotheses.</p>
    <p><dl>
      <li><title>Exact category</title><p>An abelian category has its usual exact sequences. A Quillen exact category is an additive category with a specified class of kernel–cokernel pairs satisfying exact-structure axioms; see <xref ref="ha-exact"/>. An exact category in the sense of Barr belongs to ordinary category theory and uses equivalence relations and their effective quotients. These are different uses of the same name. The full Quillen theory is developed in <xref ref="bib-ha-buehler"/>.</p></li>
      <li><title>Tensor category</title><p>We specify monoidal, symmetric, closed, linear, abelian, or rigid as separate conditions. The convention called tensor category in <xref ref="bib-ha-egno"/> includes additional conditions; its finite tensor and fusion theories are more specialized. Our basic closed structures do not require all objects to be dualizable.</p></li>
      <li><title>Hom and Ext</title><p>The group <m>\Hom_{\mathcal A}(X,Y)</m>, the complex <m>\underline{\Hom}^{\bullet}(X,Y)</m>, and an internal Hom object have distinct targets. For complexes, <m>\Ext^n(X,Y)</m> is sometimes defined to mean <m>\Hom_{\D}(X,Y[n])</m>; when this notation is used it extends the classical module- or object-valued Ext convention.</p></li>
      <li><title>Acyclic and contractible</title><p>Acyclic means zero cohomology; contractible means the identity is chain-homotopic to zero. Contractibility implies acyclicity. An acyclic complex can be nonzero in the homotopy category but is zero in the derived category.</p></li>
      <li><title>Homology, cohomology, and co-</title><p>These notes use cochain degrees, with <m>d</m> of degree <m>1</m> and <m>H^n(X)=H_{-n}(C)</m> under <m>X^n=C_{-n}</m>. This reindexing is distinct from passing to an opposite category. The conventions for resolutions, left and right derived functors, Ext, Tor, spectral sequences, and dual terminology are collected in <xref ref="ha-c00-homology-language"/>.</p></li>
      <li><title>Derived and classical derived functors</title><p>A total derived functor takes values in a derived or triangulated category. Its individual cohomology functors recover classical derived functors under the stated hypotheses. Our universal comparison for a right derived functor has direction <m>F\to\mathbf RF\,Q</m>; inspect the direction when comparing Kan-extension formulations.</p></li>
      <li><title>Six functors</title><p>Recollement involves the two adjoint triples connecting three categories. A six-operation framework involves tensor, internal Hom, and two adjoint pairs indexed by base morphisms. A recollement can express an additional localization property of a six-operation system; it does not specify that system on its own.</p></li>
    </dl></p>
  </section>
  <section xml:id="ha-concordance-hypotheses">
    <title>Conditions that must be checked separately</title>
    <p><ol>
      <li><p>The Grothendieck condition supplies enough injectives and unbounded K-injective replacements. It does not specify a tensor product, enough projectives, or compact generators for the derived category.</p></li>
      <li><p>K-flatness refers to a tensor product and an acyclic-test condition. A theorem giving K-flat replacements in modules does not by itself give them in every Grothendieck category carrying a tensor product.</p></li>
      <li><p>A bounded object has bounded cohomology. A perfect object has a bounded finite projective model. A compact object is detected by preservation of coproducts by its representable functor. A dualizable object has a tensor dual. Over a commutative ring, the last three conditions agree in the usual unbounded derived category; in an arbitrary monoidal triangulated category they require separate comparisons.</p></li>
      <li><p>A Verdier quotient is a localization at maps with prescribed cones. A right adjoint of its quotient functor, an exact localization endofunctor, and preservation of coproducts are further properties. A recollement requires additional adjoints on both sides.</p></li>
      <li><p>A natural projection or base-change comparison is not an isomorphism without a theorem or an axiom. The class of base maps and the class of squares on which invertibility is asserted are part of a six-operation framework.</p></li>
      <li><p>A formula established on an unbounded category need not preserve a bounded or finite subcategory. Restriction requires a separate boundedness, compactness, or finiteness argument.</p></li>
    </ol></p>
  </section>
</appendix>
